The eax register is a 32-bit register in x86-64 architecture. In assembly, eax is often used to hold the return value of a function.
Step 2: Read the Assembly Line by Line
Table
Address	Instruction	What it does
<+0>	endbr64	Indirect branch target — security feature, ignore for this challenge
<+4>	push rbp	Save the old base pointer (stack frame setup)
<+5>	mov rbp, rsp	Set up new base pointer (stack frame setup)
<+8>	mov DWORD PTR [rbp-0x4], edi	Store 1st function argument (32-bit integer) on stack
<+11>	mov QWORD PTR [rbp-0x10], rsi	Store 2nd function argument (64-bit pointer) on stack
<+15>	mov eax, 0x30	← THIS IS THE KEY LINE — puts 0x30 into eax
<+20>	pop rbp	Restore old base pointer (stack frame teardown)
<+21>	ret	Return to caller — eax still holds 0x30
Step 3: Apply the Hint
"Find the one line that pertains to this question and don't second guess yourself!"
The hint tells you to ignore the noise. Most of the instructions are just:
Setting up the stack frame (push rbp, mov rbp, rsp)
Storing arguments (mov ... edi, mov ... rsi)
Tearing down the stack frame (pop rbp, ret)
Only one line actually puts a value into eax:
asm
mov    eax,0x30
This is the line that answers the question.
Step 4: Convert Hex to Decimal
The value in eax is 0x30 (hexadecimal).
Conversion:
plain
0x30 = (3 × 16¹) + (0 × 16⁰)
     = 48 + 0
     = 48
Or use Python to verify:
Python
>>> 0x30
48
